Sam Dennis wrote on Fri, 13 Feb 2004 14:56:10 +0000 (UTC):
> Stanislav Traykov wrote:
>> Janis Papanagnou wrote on Fri, 13 Feb 2004 00:45:42 +0100:
>>> WINX11LIB = -L/usr/X11/lib -lXaw -lXmu -lXext -lXt -lX11
>> 
>> If you have to add that -L, it means that your system is not properly
>> configured.

> If you have to add it there, then you've misunderstood the NetHack
> makefiles (try looking at the LFLAGS lines above)

Why not add it there? Technically, it's no problem since WINLIB (which
should contain WINX11LIB if you are compiling for X11) is added to
every linker invocation (and to nothing else).

It also makes sense because only the libs (-l) in WINX11LIB depend on
this path (-L). WINQTLIB does the same in the official distribution.

> but what is bad about needing that to link to those libraries?

Adding the X11 lib directory to the system's search path is what a
sensible distro ought to do IMO (if it claims to provide a user-ready
development environment). Perhaps "not configured properly" was a bit
too strong, but refusing to do so is at least "non-standard" (in the
negative sense) by my book.

> (Other than the bad location; /lib/X11 (or, frankly, /lib) would be
> much better, but a lot of work in this case.)

I wouldn't put those files outside of the /usr hierarchy, since they
are shareable, read-only, and not "essential" in the sense that they
need to be in /. Nevertheless, if we take the FHS as guidance,
/usr/X11 is indeed not ideal (quoting the standard):

| /usr/X11R6 : X Window System, Version 11 Release 6 (optional)

| Purpose
| This hierarchy is reserved for the X Window System, version 11
| release 6, and related files.

| To simplify matters and make XFree86 more compatible with the X
| Window System on other systems, the following symbolic links must be
| present if /usr/X11R6 exists:

|     /usr/bin/X11 -> /usr/X11R6/bin
|     /usr/lib/X11 -> /usr/X11R6/lib/X11
|     /usr/include/X11 -> /usr/X11R6/include/X11

| In general, software must not be installed or managed via the above
| symbolic links. They are intended for utilization by users only. The
| difficulty is related to the release version of the X Window
| System--in transitional periods, it is impossible to know what
| release of X11 is in use.
[...]

